<table cellpadding="3" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%" align="center" class="tborder" style="border-bottom: 0;">
	<tr class="titlebg">
		<td valign="middle" align="left" width="15%" style="padding-left: 6px;">
			<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/topic/normal_post.gif" alt="" />
			Author
		</td>
		<td valign="middle" align="left" width="85%" style="padding-left: 6px;">
			Topic: Chapter 6 &nbsp;(Read 113 times)
		</td>
	</tr>
</table>
<table cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%" class="bordercolor">
	<tr><td style="padding: 1px 1px 0 1px;">
		<table cellpadding="3" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%">
			<tr><td class="windowbg">
				<table width="100%" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0">
					<tr>
						<td valign="top" width="15%" rowspan="2">
							<b><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?action=profile;u=81" title="View the profile of Phoebie">Phoebie</a></b><br />
							<span class="smalltext">
								Orc Pawn<br />
								<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/star.gif" alt="*" border="0" /><br />
								Posts: 15<br />
								<br />
								<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/YaBBImages/avatars/blank.gif" alt="" border="0" /><br />
								I'm a llama!<br />
								<br />
								
								
								
								<br />
								<a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?action=profile;u=81"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/icons/profile_sm.gif" alt="View Profile" title="View Profile" border="0" /></a>
								<a href="mailto:brneydmzo@bellsouth.net"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/email_sm.gif" alt="Email" title="Email" border="0" /></a>
							</span>
						</td>
						<td valign="top" width="85%" height="100%">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=120.msg375#msg375"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=120.msg375#msg375">Chapter 6</a></b><br />
									<span class="smalltext">&#171; <b> on:</b> April 10, 2003, 02:04:16 pm &#187;</span></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							The high elf winced as the familiar smell of the vegetation flooded his nostrils. His memories of this place were bittersweet, and not ones he brought to the surface of his conscience frequently; these memories pained him with their potency and reminded him of what, and more specifically, who he had lost.<br /><br />Despite the pangs of nostalgia, he had missed this place so much, and without reason he had suddenly been complelled to drop his studies in Felewithe and journey through the Wakening Lands to the Portal of Growth, inexplicably drawn to the home of his goddess, some force beyond his control beckoning him with an urgency it was impossible to ignore.<br /><br />The grass was emerald green and springy beneath his feet, and the whooshing sound of air through moist nostrils could be heard to his left as a pack of rolling plains steeds meandered by, their heads rising only slightly to acknowledge his arrival and returning almost immediately to their furious grazing. &nbsp;The sky was as blue as topaz, the air crisp and scented with the smell of hundreds of flowers and shrubs, and he could hear the lilting song of birds overhead as he adjusted his backpack and started heading North towards the Tree of Tunare. &nbsp;It had been nineteen seasons ago when he had first stepped foot into this place, his eagerness for adventure lulling him here perhaps before he should have come. &nbsp;Along with a wandering band of adventurers, they had set out across the icy tundras of Velious, through the towering city of Kael Drakkal, and into the dense overgrown forests of the Wakening Lands, running from panthers and giant looming suits of armor as they made their way slowly but surely to the towering stairs on the island in the lake, guarded by fauns and fairies, leading to the Plane of Growth.<br /><br />Crestus took a long, deep breath, the air noticeably heavy as he breathed it, moist with dew or perhaps a recent rainfall. &nbsp;His feet travelled without prompting, he had memorized this journey after making it dozens of times alone or in a group, and as he swerved slightly west and along the river, he felt that compelling whisper once again welling up in his soul, and he quickened his feet to reach the tree; something was not right, and his curiosity was now replaced with a looming sense of urgency and danger. &nbsp;He wiped the beading perspiration from his forehead with the back of his hand, took an eager swig from his water flask, and grabbed a handful of berries off of a heavily-laden wild raspberry bush as he walked by. &nbsp;A small bluebird chirped her protest as he grabbed the exact clump of fruit she was intending to grab for herself, and with the impatient fluttering of wings and a series of chirps that sounded almost like a scolding, she flew off deeper into the undergrowth to find more.<br /><br />He continued to travel, his breathing becoming heavier, almost a pant, as he sucked the moist air into his lungs feverishly. &nbsp;Perhaps it was not rain that weighted the atmosphere, perhaps those were the lingering tears of Tunare as she cried for the loss of someone, or something. The sense of urgency grew to a screaming swell, tearing at his heart, screaming down into his very soul, hastening his steps even more despite the burning protests of his lungs and aching muscles. &nbsp;Starting to jog slightly, Crestus whispered a small prayer to his Goddess, asking her for strength and wisdom that he sensed he would need very soon.<br /><br />***************<br /><br />She looked weary and worn, but even her apparent exhaustion was not enough to shroud her overwhelming beauty. &nbsp;He stared at her in awe, it had been ten years since he had seen her, but he knew it was her, unmistakably. &nbsp;Karadana. &nbsp;He whispered it gently, to himself, her name like a forbidden prayer on his lips.<br /><br />She was standing around a fire with several others, grasping the legendary Spear of Fate in her hand; he wondered when she had received that, he remembered she had always spoken of this weapon with awe, it was one of the highest honors the Tribunal could grant to a shaman, and she looked good holding it. &nbsp;He made a mental note to congratulate her later, that is, if he could even muster up the stones to speak to her.<br /><br />She knelt down next to the fire and pulled some bandages out of her knapsack, her hands moving deftly as she smeared some sort of pumace onto them from a vial, and stood up to look around for someone, perhaps the lucky recipient of her ministrations. &nbsp;She glanced his way briefly, not recognizing him, then began to walk in the other direction. &nbsp;His heart fell to the soles of his feet, heavy with disappointment, but then she stopped moving, and slowly turned around to face him. &nbsp;Her eyes were wide with shock, her mouth working almost in slow motion to form the word.<br /><br />&quot;P..P...Puppy?&quot;<br /><br />He winced as he heard that too-familiar nickname, she was the only one who called him that, and in a way he was surprised she even remembered. &nbsp;He had a sudden urge to turn around and run in the other direction, and it took all of his courage to stand there and face her as she approached, her arms open wide. He strained to put a smile on his face, and his arms felt like lead as he tried to raise them to receive her embrace.<br /><br />She hugged him long and hard, the scent of her hair unmistakable, and he closed his eyes and drew a deep breath, almost crying with the joy of having her in his arms again. She had grown even taller and more shapely, he could feel her pressing up against him with her womanly fullness, yet her waist was still slender and he could have easily wrapped his arms around her almost twice. She was thin, too thin, and he could feel the poking bones of her ribcage even through her heavy skins. Perhaps the life of an adventurer was not as easy and prosperous as he imagined; Karadana had never been short on appetite, but he could fathom no other reason for her timid frame other than her missing one too many meals.<br /><br />&quot;You are the last person I ever expected to see today, here...what...how did you....&quot;<br /><br />She collapsed again into his arms, this time showering his cheeks with warm kisses, her tears coming suddenly and rolling down her face onto his shoulder and neck as she embraced him. &nbsp;He pulled back, looking deeply into her ageless eyes, and was suddenly overwhelmed by passion. He grasped her face, and felt her skin beneath his rough fingers, and as he pulled her close to kiss her, she resisted.<br /><br />&quot;No...no Kaidog...&quot;<br /><br />She pointed down to her left ring finger, and he noticed it encircled in a thin platinum band, one engraved with runes so worn they were almost indetectable, but their message was piercingly clear; &quot;...married on this 19th day of July, the year 3178...&quot;<br /><br />Married. &nbsp;She was married. &nbsp;<br /><br />His heart felt like a burning stone in his chest, his cheeks flushed with sudden embarassment, his eyes falling shyly to the ground. How could he have been such a fool? Of course she was married, girls like that didn't stay single for long. He wondered who the man was he would now be forced to hate, what petulent warrior-wannabe child or snobby rich heir to a fortune had snapped up his beloved? &nbsp;His sense of loss and embarassment suddenly turned to all-consuming jealousy, and he dropped his arms casually to his side and tried to be nonchalant.<br /><br />&quot;Aaah, yes...married...well. Congratulations.&quot;<br /><br />She had always known him well, perhaps too well, and her hand stretched out to stroke his scruffy chin.<br /><br />&quot;You don't understand...it was not a marriage of love, Puppy. It was for reasons I will explain to you later...reasons I am ashamed of, but I will nonetheless respect, because I am a woman of honor. &nbsp;But believe me when I say that my reasons were strong, and I only hope someday that you, and my heart, can forgive me...&quot;<br /><br />She smiled at him, wiped away one last tear from her cheek, and squared her shoulders with resolve.<br /><br />&quot;I must be off to find my husband, he has injured himself and I must minister to his needs. I will find you later tonight, and we will catch up over a meal. &nbsp;I have missed you so, and I am glad you are here.&quot;<br /><br />She strode off across the dark ground, and he could feel the echo of his breaking heart with each step she took.
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" colspan="2" class="smalltext">
								</td>
							</tr><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
								</td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
									<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/ip.gif" alt="" border="0" /> Logged
								</td>
							</tr></table>
						</td>
					</tr>
				</table>
			</td></tr>
		</table>
	</td></tr>
</table>
